findings.description:
Context: `AsyncMatching.addOrder` checks minimum order size before `AsyncFiller.addOrder` escrows the priority bid. For sells, the required bid is `minPriorityFee(amountOutMin)`: the lesser of 0.1% of the minimum output and 100,000 USDC base units ($0.10). Makers can subsequently reprice through `AsyncSwap.updatePrice` or authorize a relayer through `updatePriceWithSig`; both reach `AsyncFiller.updatePrice`. Bids are non-refundable: direct matches settle them proportionally, while fills and cancellations settle the remaining bid to `FeeVault`.

```solidity
checkMinimum(self, book, order.key.toId(), info.isBuy, info.zeroForOne, info.amountIn, newAmountOutMin);
uint256 old = info.amountOutMin;
_remove(self, book, id);
info.amountOutMin = newAmountOutMin;
info.price = _priceKey(info.isBuy, info.amountIn, newAmountOutMin);
```

Root cause: `AsyncFiller.updatePrice` validates ownership, order liveness, a positive new minimum, and `checkMinimum`, but never checks whether the retained `priorityFee` satisfies `minPriorityFee(newAmountOutMin)`. It changes the sell's minimum output and price and reinserts the order with fresh time priority without increasing its escrowed bid. Both direct and signed repricing inherit this omission.

With `book.minOrder == 0`, including pools initialized while `defaultMinOrder` remains unset, a maker can create a sell with `amountOutMin = 1` or `999` USDC base units and `priorityFee = 0`, because the tiered bid rounds down to zero. Repricing to `100_000_000` base units (100 USDC), or to 1,000 USDC, retains that zero bid, although either final notional requires 100,000 base units at admission. A nonzero minimum can still allow partial underpayment: under the deployment's configured 10-USDC minimum, an initial 10-USDC sell requires 10,000 base units ($0.01), but repricing to 1,000 USDC does not collect the additional 90,000 base units ($0.09).

Through the ordinary Router route, the initially cheap order must survive placement matching—for example, with no opposing buy or active LP liquidity. After obtaining its assigned salt, the maker can call `updatePrice`, or anyone can relay a valid maker-signed `updatePriceWithSig` for the live order.

Alternatively, a maker controlling a custom executor can perform the bypass atomically. A maker contract can authorize itself through `setExecutor`, create and fund the order through `PoolManager.swap` during an unlock, and call `updatePrice` before the separate `matchNewOrder` call. `_beforeSwap` creates the order but does not match it. The executor may then permit matching or omit `matchNewOrder` and leave the repriced order resting, so this route does not expose the temporary low minimum to execution.

Context: `Router.cancelWithSig` lets anyone holding a maker's EIP-712 `CancelOrder` signature cancel that maker's order. The digest commits to the maker, the directional tokens, the `orderId`, the live stored `amountIn`, a maker-scoped `nonce` and a `deadline`. Replay is meant to be blocked by `cancelNonceUsed[maker][nonce]`. `OrderSignatures.hashCancel` states the nonce exists because "order ids can recur (identical order re-placed in the same block after a cancel), so order deletion alone does not stop a signed cancel from being replayed." The project summary also says makers can "Invalidate unused signature nonces (`AsyncSwap.invalidateNonce`)".

```solidity
function cancelWithSig(AsyncOrder calldata order, uint256 nonce, uint256 deadline, bytes calldata signature) external {
    if (block.timestamp > deadline) revert SignatureExpired();
    if (cancelNonceUsed[order.owner][nonce]) revert NonceAlreadyUsed();
    cancelNonceUsed[order.owner][nonce] = true;
    bytes32 id = _orderId(order);
    (uint256 amountIn,) = HOOK.asyncOrderInfo(order.key.toId(), id);
    bytes32 digest = _hashTypedDataV4(OrderSignatures.hashCancel(order, id, amountIn, nonce, deadline));
```

Root cause: `cancelNonceUsed` is written in exactly one place: inside `cancelWithSig` itself. The Router has no function that lets a maker mark a cancel nonce as used or revoke a signed cancel. `AsyncSwap.invalidateNonce` lives in the hook's own storage. The Router never reads it, so it cannot affect Router cancel nonces.

Several paths close an order without consuming the outstanding signed-cancel nonce: a direct `cancelOrder`, a full `fillOrder`, a full book match, a pool-liquidity fill. After any of these, a previously issued `CancelOrder` signature stays valid until `deadline`. `updatePrice` does not invalidate it either, because the digest binds `orderId` and `amountIn`, not `amountOutMin`. The nonce therefore only prevents replay when the cancel was executed through `cancelWithSig`. That is narrower than the replay protection the `hashCancel` comment describes.
